3. SOURCE-OF-TRUTH AND EVIDENCE HIERARCHY
When sources disagree, do not silently choose whichever description looks most convincing.
Use this hierarchy:
1. Actual source code and its reachable execution paths.
2. Reproducible runtime behavior and executable test results.
3. Directly inspected model metadata, data, and configuration.
4. Test definitions and test fixtures.
5. Current project documentation.
6. Historical reports and previous audit claims.
This hierarchy does not mean that source code alone proves correct behavior. A code path may exist but may never execute, may receive invalid inputs, or may produce incorrect results.
Maintain the following distinctions:
Implemented: A real implementation exists.
Reachable: A real application path can invoke it.
Executed: The behavior has actually been executed in the inspected environment.
Verified: Evidence appropriate to the claimed behavior demonstrates that it works correctly.
Never use these terms interchangeably.
A successful model initialization is not proof of accurate transcription.
A successful test compilation is not proof of runtime correctness.
A successful audio ingestion test is not proof of end-to-end integration.

